<description>
Expand the fill-color corner around a single marker into two large diagonal blocks.

Input structure
- The input is a 3x3 grid with one background color.
- The two foreground colors are a single marker pixel and a three-pixel fill color.
- The marker and fill pixels occupy a 2x2 square, with the marker at one corner.

Rule
- Find the diagonal direction from the marker to the opposite fill pixel.
- Make a 9x9 background canvas.
- Paint one 4x4 fill-color block in the matching output corner and another
  4x4 fill-color block one step inward along that diagonal.

Key insight
- The missing corner of the 2x2 fill square is the marker; its orientation
  chooses which diagonal staircase of two enlarged blocks is drawn.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "3x3 grid containing a 2x2 foreground corner: one marker pixel and the other three cells in the fill color",
  "transform": "replace the marker/fill corner with a 9x9 bg canvas containing two 4x4 fill-color blocks along the diagonal selected by the marker-to-opposite-fill direction",
  "grid_shape": "9x9"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  input:
    grid: 3x3
    background: one color, 5 cells
    foreground: 2 colors -- fill (3 cells) and marker (1 cell)
    structure: fill + marker form a 2x2 block occupying one of 4 quadrants of the 3x3 grid; marker is always at the inner corner (row 1, col 1); fill occupies the other 3 cells of that 2x2
    colors: bg=0, marker=2, fill=3 [observed]; solver only requires 3 distinct colors
  output:
    grid: 9x9, same background color
    content: two 4x4 fill-color blocks arranged diagonally
    placement: |
      diagonal direction = marker-to-opposite-fill-corner (dr, dc in {-1,+1})
      block1 at the output corner along that diagonal
      block2 one 4x4 step inward (shifted by 4*(-dr), 4*(-dc) from block1)
      e.g. dr=1,dc=1 -> block1 at (5,5)-(8,8), block2 at (1,1)-(4,4)
    fill_cell_count: 32 (2 x 16)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (input is tiny and fully constrained; no output-first needed)
  steps:
    1. Pick 3 distinct colors for bg, fill, marker
    2. Choose one of 4 diagonal directions (dr, dc) in {-1,+1}x{-1,+1}
    3. Build 3x3 bg grid; place marker at (1,1); fill the 3 cells (1+dr,1), (1,1+dc), (1+dr,1+dc) with fill color
```
